        "category": "3. Experienced / Advanced Level (101–150) - Event Loop & Architecture",
        "language": "javascript",
        "explanation": "When you use the `.pipe()` method (or `pipeline`), Node.js handles backpressure automatically. \n1. If the writable stream's internal buffer gets full, `.write()` returns `false`.\n2. The `.pipe()` mechanism sees this, and tells the readable stream to `pause()` reading from the file.\n3. Once the writable stream finishes writing the backlog to the network, it emits a `drain` event.\n4. `.pipe()` hears the `drain` event and tells the readable stream to `resume()` reading.",
        "example": "Manual Backpressure Handling (What pipe does under the hood).",
        "code": "readStream.on('data', (chunk) => {\n    // If buffer is full, write() returns false\n    const canContinue = writeStream.write(chunk);\n    if (!canContinue) {\n        readStream.pause(); // Stop reading!\n    }\n});\n\n// When the write stream has caught up, it emits 'drain'\nwriteStream.on('drain', () => {\n    readStream.resume(); // Start reading again!\n});",
        "tip": "Always use `.pipe()` or `pipeline` to avoid having to write this complex pause/resume logic yourself.",
